Batch pipeline benchmark -- 8 variants over a shared prefix, 200,000 rows
Objective (paper 4.3): minimize execution time under memory constraints,
so both wall-clock and peak RSS are reported.

Two constructions of the same batch, differing only in how the
redundant algebra is distributed across the eight variants.

[A] uniform redundancy -- every variant carries the SAME redundant algebra
  CSE   rewrites     ops   optimize ms   peak RSS MB
  off   off          102         85.87        405.2
  off   on            53         90.90        404.8
  on    off           28         92.44        404.9
  on    on            21         86.45        404.9
    CSE alone             : 102 ->  28 ops (74 removed)
    rewrites alone        : 102 ->  53 ops (49 removed)
    rewrites on top of CSE:  28 ->  21 ops ( 7 removed)   <-- our contribution

[B] varied redundancy -- each variant carries a DIFFERENT pattern
  CSE   rewrites     ops   optimize ms   peak RSS MB
  off   off           68         63.90        349.9
  off   on            53         56.34        349.9
  on    off           33         60.75        350.0
  on    on            21         60.44        350.2
    CSE alone             :  68 ->  33 ops (35 removed)
    rewrites alone        :  68 ->  53 ops (15 removed)
    rewrites on top of CSE:  33 ->  21 ops (12 removed)   <-- our contribution

Why the two differ
  rewrites' contribution on top of CSE:  [A] 7 ops   [B] 12 ops
  In [A] the redundancy is identical across variants, so CSE collapses
  it to one copy before the rewrites run -- they then have only that
  copy left to eliminate. In [B] the redundancy differs per variant, so
  CSE cannot dedup it and each pattern must be rewritten on its own.
  [B] is the more realistic model of agent output and isolates the
  rewrites' own contribution; [A] understates it by construction.
